{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the GitHub connector's outbound half at exact clean head 68c0f42fee192e24bc194ec262cad359e4784fd3, directly citing artifact 4eb2dc81fd837b4cd7e3ad5b8aeaf21e096ff12e. Do not merge.\n\nPriority 6 of 6, last deliberately: one of its conditions is unmet and cannot be met by me, so approving it does not release anything until Hugh acts. Review it when the five above are clear.\n\nA pull request is a rendering rather than a decision. Its body names the governing request, the artifact and the exact head under review, and says plainly that approval and merge happen in gitseq — `gs merge` refuses anything that is not the approved commit. It carries identifiers rather than a summary, because a summary is a second copy of the record that can drift from the first.\n\nOpening one is a command rather than a projection, because a pull request cannot be idempotently overwritten and a second call makes a second one. It reports the created number and URL for a report to carry as evidence, and fails both when GitHub refuses and when GitHub accepts and returns nothing usable — the second case would otherwise leave a promise looking kept with no evidence able to close it.\n\nThe read-back defence arrives with the fetch that makes it reachable, which is why deleting it from the inbound half was right. A test renders a proposal, feeds the result back through the inbound half exactly as GitHub would return it, and shows no act is produced; the rendering is excluded twice, as a pull request and as our own writing. Worth attacking: I claim in the code that the marker is not a security boundary, only a loop bound between two halves of one connector. If you think that comment overclaims or underclaims, say so.\n\nThe unmet condition: the end-to-end dogfood on a real issue has not happened. It opens a pull request on a public repository, which I cannot undo by superseding an event, and it needs an outbound charter. The one that existed was empty-bodied and premised on a charter Hugh has since retired, so I withdrew it rather than leave a doorstep the connector refuses. A scoped one has to be stated and ratified by an operator.\n\nGates: `go test ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/ -count=1` passes, `go vet ./...` clean. Verified against the live workroom: a retired charter is refused with \"charter is retired\", an incomplete proposal with \"--commit is required with --propose\".","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4eb2dc81fd837b4cd7e3ad5b8aeaf21e096ff12e","conditions":"A verdict of approved or changes-requested against exact clean head 68c0f42fee192e24bc194ec262cad359e4784fd3, resting directly on artifact 4eb2dc81fd837b4cd7e3ad5b8aeaf21e096ff12e, with body.verdict set; the judgement says whether the connector can write anything without a live charter naming it, whether the read-back defence holds by construction, whether the marker comment states its limits correctly, and whether approving with the end-to-end condition unmet is the right disposition or the head should wait for the charter. Do not merge.","head":"68c0f42fee192e24bc194ec262cad359e4784fd3","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}